    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the course was planned by AI agents from research notes on the letters, the syllable blocks, the history and design of Hangul, the consonants, the final sounds and the sound changes, read from South Korea's language norms, the National Institute of Korean Language, the Unicode Character Database, Wikipedia and Wiktionary; an outline was drafted from the notes and reviewed, and the chapters were then written from it in English and translated from the English into Swedish, German, Spanish and French. The working notes, the outline and the glossaries are kept with the course's sources, not in the library."@en ,
                 "The course was checked by machine (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks, the course's draft checker for the same structure and theory chunks in every language, and a second SHACL validator) and by AI agents against the sources; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Selection: what a beginner needs to read Hangul aloud: what a syllable block is and the order to read it in, and where the script comes from; the six simple vowels, ㅐ and ㅔ and the y-vowels; the five basic consonant shapes, the letters made from them and the plain, aspirated and tense series; where each vowel goes in a block, the silent ㅇ, the combined vowels and ㅢ; the seven sounds a syllable can end in and which letter of a double final is read; and the regular sound changes between blocks: linking, nasalization, nasalization of ㄹ and lateralization, aspiration, palatalization and the clear cases of tensification, each cited by its article in the Standard Pronunciation rules. Every sound is given in IPA, simplified from Wiktionary's narrow forms, and each language has its own approximate hints for the sounds, written for that language rather than translated; since cards have no audio, questions ask about letters, blocks and rules, not how a sound feels. Every example word asked about was checked on English Wiktionary. Left out on purpose: romanization, which is only mentioned; vowel length, which most speakers no longer keep; irregular double finals (such as 읽다 and 밟다) and the less predictable cases of tensification, mentioned but not asked; and soft numbers, such as how many syllables are in real use, as things to learn."@en ,
                 "Structure: 6 chapters of 22 steps in all. Each step is a short theory text in Markdown, read in two or three chunks, sometimes with a table, and two or three multiple-choice questions on it; each chapter has two review questions that combine its steps and are asked only in its final review: 45 step questions and 12 review questions, 57 questions in all. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors) of similar length and form, each with a note on why it is wrong. Every text is in English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French; the Korean words, the Hangul in brackets, the letters and the IPA are the same in every language."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0, like most of the library. Its theory, questions, options, notes and hints are in the course's own words; no text is copied from any source. The Standard Pronunciation rules and Hangul Orthography show no license on their official pages; as notices of the state, their text is not protected under Article 7 of the Korean Copyright Act, and the course still restates them in its own words and cites them by article number. The National Institute of Korean Language's English pages are all rights reserved or show no license, and only facts were taken from them. Wikipedia and Wiktionary are CC BY-SA 4.0; only facts were taken and examples checked, nothing was copied, so their share-alike terms do not reach the course. From the Unicode Character Database only character names and code points were used, as facts; no data file was copied."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the whole release, with the library checker (npm run library:check), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, and checks the course by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release; with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes; and with the course's draft checker, which compares every translation with the English for the same steps, questions, options and Markdown flags and the same number of theory chunks."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the release conforms to every shape, profile and rule the checker applies to it, and the crosscheck agrees."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Outline: facts and question design (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the outline checked by two AI agents, one that tried to refute every teaching point, answer, wrong option and example word against the research notes and the primary sources (the official Standard Pronunciation text, Korean Wikisource, Wikipedia and Wiktionary), and one for the questions' design (one answer, recall from the front alone, options of the same form, nothing taught before it is needed); not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 48 findings (21 on facts, 27 on design), all fixed in the outline before the chapters were written, one of them in part (a third review question per chapter was not added, since the format has two); among them linking limited to particles when the examples were inside words, a letter-name history and a vowel's sound hint that went further than the sources, two questions that tested the same fact, wrong options that were never taught, and pronunciation fronts that did not say whether the answer is in Hangul or IPA. Every article number the outline cites was confirmed in the current official text."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter text in English: facts, design and language (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter as written, reviewed by two AI agents: one for every fact, example word, answer and wrong option against the research and the sources, checking words the research lacked on English Wiktionary; one for the questions' design, each front read alone first, and for the English and the Markdown; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 68 findings across the six chapters (38 on facts, 30 on design and language), all fixed in the text; among them claims that went further than the rules (a final said to move before any vowel, where the rule needs an ending or particle), a final sound [ㅈ] that does not exist, a wrong option that could be defended from the theory, the claim that the tongue closes the mouth, terms used before they were taught, and an unlabeled English sound hint."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Course as a whole in English (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the six chapters read together by an AI agent for terms used differently, facts taught twice or contradicted, forward references, duplicate questions, backs that stand out from the wrong options, and fronts that do not work as flip cards; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 7 findings, all fixed: three terms used differently between chapters (two-letter, double and doubled finals or letters), two names for the same commentary, the Standard Pronunciation rules cited before they were introduced, a notation introduced twice as new, and a review question with two defensible answers from its front alone."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Translations into Swedish, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: for each language, a glossary of the course's terms and new sound hints for that language drawn up first by an AI agent; each chapter translated from the English by an AI agent, then reviewed by more, one for fidelity, translating the text back into English and comparing it with the English, and one as a native editor for natural language, terminology and typography; then each language's six chapters read together by an AI agent for consistency; the title, description and keywords written in all six app languages; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 206 findings across the 24 chapter translations (Swedish 66, German 58, Spanish 39, French 43), all fixed in the text; the consistency pass then found and fixed 25 more (Swedish 10, German 4, Spanish 6, French 5), evening out terms, glosses and voice within each language."@en .

